      "id": "ES-C43-comida",
      "sequence": 1193,
      "title": "la comida — \"food,\" built from a verb you have",
      "headword": "la comida",
      "romanization": "la comida",
      "gloss": "food, a meal — a noun you can build yourself out of a verb you already own",
      "script": "latin",
      "modality": "voice",
      "derivedModality": "voice",
      "modalityReasons": [
        "no-visual-dependency"
      ],
      "sourceHash": "fnv1a64:e757e6aa0e96ff99",
      "notice": null,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "index": 0,
          "type": "warmup",
          "title": "Warm-up",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "pause",
              "seconds": 2,
              "perItem": false,
              "source": "[PAUSE 2s]"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Say \"to eat.\" (Comer.) Keep it in mind — this chapter's noun is made out of it, in front of you."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "pronunciation",
          "title": "Sounds you'll need",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "d-soft — the d between vowels is soft, close to the th in English this: ko-MEE-thah, not a hard d."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "la comida — food, and also a meal."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Take comer, drop the ending, add -ida:"
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "comer becomes comida — literally the eaten thing."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "That ending is not decoration. It is a working tool: it takes a verb and hands you back a noun for the result of that verb. You will meet it again and recognise it when you do."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "This is the first Spanish noun you did not have to be given. You could have built it. That is a different kind of possession from memorising a list, and it is the reason the ending is worth more here than the word."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Comer itself comes from Latin comedere — ed- is \"eat,\" the same ancient root as English eat, with com- (\"thoroughly\") in front. So comida is, at the bottom, \"the thoroughly-eaten thing.\""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "grammar",
          "title": "Grammar Lens: -ida nouns are feminine",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Nouns built this way end in -a and are feminine, every time. So the ending gives you two things at once: the word, and its gender."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "la comida — the food. Hago la comida. — I make the meal."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Set your three nouns side by side and the pattern is doing all the work:"
            },
            {
              "kind": "table",
              "headers": [
                "noun",
                "gender",
                "article"
              ],
              "columns": 3,
              "rowCount": 3,
              "utterances": [
                "noun: libro. gender: masculine. article: el.",
                "noun: casa. gender: feminine. article: la.",
                "noun: comida. gender: feminine. article: la."
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
